From 120b27356b6081a1df59804d5221eb83cdf3f24e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HYDRA session <hydra@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:32:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Session 179b: tiles 3x4 -> 4x4; v2 does not fit the v1 tile
 size

Measured with yosys+abc against sky130_fd_sc_hd (tools/sky130_area.sh in the
hydra-skywater130 repository): v1 at b179c6b is 135,203 um2 of standard cells,
v2 is 194,647 um2 -- 44% more, from the SPI target, the register block, the
128-bit descriptor staging and the read multiplexer.

A 3x4 tile is 216,432 um2 (167 x 108 um each), so v2 would need ~90% density
where v1 hardened at ~62%. 4x4 (288,576 um2) puts v2 at 67.5%.

Dropping the LASTWD readback saves only 8% (178,262 um2) and does not rescue
3x4, so the readback stays.

These are not OpenLane numbers -- that flow synthesises differently and adds
buffering, clock tree and fill. The ratio is the result; confirm with a real
harden.
---
 info.yaml | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/info.yaml b/info.yaml
index 07dfe71..9c15799 100644
--- a/info.yaml
+++ b/info.yaml
@@ -80,7 +80,8 @@ project:
   # three cycles per descriptor either way; nothing about the design cares.
   clock_hz:     18000000
 
-  tiles: "3x4"
+  tiles: "4x4"          # v2 is 44% larger than v1 (135,203 -> 194,647 um2 of
+                        # cells, tools/sky130_area.sh): 3x4 would need ~90% density
 
   top_module:  "tt_um_hydra_mom"
 
